2026-10-04 23:31:53,651 gage.orchestrator INFO answer trace {"farm": 1, "language": "kn", "question": "ಪ್ರತಿ irrigation ಗೆ ಎಷ್ಟು cm water ಹಾಕ್ಬೇಕು?", "retrieval_queries": ["ಪ್ರತಿ irrigation ಗೆ ಎಷ್ಟು cm water ಹಾಕ್ಬೇಕು?", "How many cubic decimeters of water are required for each irrigation?", "How many cm of water per irrigation for sugarcane?"], "model_input": {"context": "You are Gage, an experienced agricultural field officer who advises sugarcane farmers in Karnataka (Kannada and English). You reason like an agronomist who has walked thousands of fields: practical, evidence-first, never guessing.\n\nAnswer EVERY question using exactly these four sections, in this order:\nObservation: what the data currently shows — Observed Facts only (sensor numbers, trends, alerts). No interpretation here.\nAnalysis: what those facts most likely indicate — this is Inference; keep it clearly separate from the facts above.\nConfidence: exactly one of High, Medium, or Low, with a one-line reason.\nRecommendations: three labelled parts —\n  - Immediate actions\n  - Monitoring advice\n  - When to seek expert help\n\nHard rules:\n- Never mix Observed Facts, Inference, and Recommendation.\n- State only numbers (amounts, doses, ranges, depths, dates, prices, days) that appear in AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE, in the farm data, or in the question. Never make up a number or a range.\n- Quote farm readings exactly as given (same value, same unit).\n- Never call a reading good, bad, low, high or optimal unless AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE gives the range that makes it so; otherwise just report the value.\n- When you name an input (for example gypsum, ferrous sulphate, urea), say only what AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE says it is for.\n- If there are active alerts, address them FIRST.\n- Gage does not analyse photos. Never claim to have seen the crop; name a disease only as a possibility consistent with the sensor readings and the symptoms the farmer describes, and recommend an in-person inspection to confirm.\n- Prefer advice that relies on the farm's own observations, not external weather.\n- Reply in the farmer's language: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\nQUESTION TYPE: knowledge (a general farming question, not about this farm's current state).\n- Answer it from AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE. In Observation, state what the text says that answers the question; farm readings are optional context.\n- Refuse only when the text does not contain the answer: then say \"The reference text does not cover this.\" and name exactly what is missing. Do not refuse because farm readings are missing — this question does not need them.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (irrigation)\nJudge irrigation timing from the soil-moisture level and its trend and the crop stage. Say whether to irrigate now, wait, or hold, and why.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: 12.806987279716143, 74.93193321230041\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC\n- Temperature: 27.9 C\n- Humidity: 72.4 %\n- Soil moisture: 32.3 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nOnly one observation on record — no comparison possible yet.\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [critical] Node offline (no contact for 49550 min)\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Summary:\nSugarcane is a long-duration, water-intensive crop requiring 1,500–2,500 mm of water over its growing cycle depending on climate. IISR documents phase-wise irrigation schedules, state-wise irrigation requirements, and three water-economizing techniques (skip-furrow, sprinkler, drip) that can cut water use by 30–95% while maintaining or improving productivity — highly relevant for Karnataka, which has one of the highest irrigation-water requirements among sugarcane-growing states in India.\n\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Key Facts:\n- Total seasonal water requirement: 1,500–2,500 mm, depending on climate.\n- Karnataka irrigation requirement: 32 irrigations/season; 266 kg water per kg sugar produced.\n- Skip-furrow irrigation: 30–40% water saving.\n- Sprinkler irrigation: 50–60% water economy; drip irrigation: up to 95% application uniformity.\n- Recommended irrigation depth per application: 7–8 cm.\n\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Main Information:\n- General guidance: irrigation water depth of 7–8 cm per application is recommended, with frequency varying by crop phase — approximately once every 7 days during germination (1–35 days after planting, DAP), once every 10 days during tillering (36–100 DAP), once every 7 days during grand growth (101–270 DAP), and once every 15 days during maturity (271 DAP to harvest), adjusted for rainfall.\n- Subtropical regions typically need 6–8 irrigations across the season; tropical regions (including Karnataka) may need 20–36 irrigations due to climatic conditions, annual rainfall, planting season, crop duration, and soil type.\n\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Main Information:\n- **Karnataka-specific data**: 32 irrigations required per season; average yield 85.5 t/ha; irrigation water required to produce 1.0 kg of sugar is 266 kg — among the highest of all sugarcane states reported, reflecting Karnataka's tropical multi-irrigation cropping pattern.\n- **Net irrigation water requirement by technique (Karnataka, mm/year)**: furrow irrigation 1,878 mm; skip furrow 1,408 mm; sprinkler 1,325 mm; drip 1,186 mm — showing drip irrigation can reduce net water requirement by roughly 37% compared to conventional furrow irrigation for the same crop water demand.\n\n### Water-economizing methods\n1. **Skip-furrow/alternate furrow irrigation**: one row is skipped and irrigation applied only to alternate furrows, saving 30–40% water without reducing cane productivity.\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns